1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
In this section, we'll explain what OS command injection is, describe how vulnerabilities

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,880
can be detected and exploited, spell out some useful commands and techniques for different

3
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:23,520
operating systems, and summarize how to prevent OS command injection.

4
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:28,320
OS command injection, also known as shell injection, is a web security vulnerability

5
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:33,440
that allows an attacker to execute arbitrary operating system commands on the server that

6
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:39,160
is running an application, and typically fully compromise the application and all its data.

7
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:44,200
Very often, an attacker can leverage an OS command injection vulnerability to compromise

8
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,980
other parts of the hosting infrastructure, exploiting trust relationships to pivot the

9
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:56,260
attack to other systems within the organization.

10
00:00:56,260 --> 00:01:01,080
Consider a shopping application that lets the user view whether an item is in stock in

11
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,640
a particular store.

12
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:08,200
This information is accessed via a URL like this.

13
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:14,080
To provide the stock information, the application must query various legacy systems.

14
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:19,100
For historical reasons, the functionality is implemented by calling out to a shell command

15
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:22,700
with the product and store IDs as arguments.

16
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:28,860
This command outputs the stock status for the specified item, which returned to the user.

17
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:34,540
Since the application implements no defenses against OS command injection, an attacker can submit

18
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:38,560
this input to execute an arbitrary command.

19
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:43,420
If the input is submitted in the product ID parameter, then the application will execute this

20
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:45,240
command.

21
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:50,680
The echo command simply causes the supplied string to be echoed in the output and is a

22
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:54,760
useful way to test for some types of OS command injection.

23
00:01:54,760 --> 00:02:00,360
The ampersand character is a shell command separator, and so what gets executed is actually three

24
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:03,100
separate commands one after another.

25
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:07,520
As a result, this output is returned to the user.

26
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:13,060
From the output, we can see that the original command was executed without its expected arguments,

27
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:16,080
and so returned an error message.

28
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:21,520
We can see that the injected echo command was executed, and the supplied string was echoed

29
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:23,760
in the output.

30
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:31,760
And we can see that the original argument, 29, was executed as a command, which caused an error.

31
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:36,980
Embracing the additional command separator after the injected command is generally useful because

32
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:41,360
it separates the injected command from whatever follows the injection point.

33
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:49,920
This reduces the likelihood that what follows will prevent the injected command from executing.

34
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:55,620
When you have identified an OS command injection vulnerability, it is generally useful to execute

35
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:00,980
some initial commands to obtain information about the system that you have compromised.

36
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:11,100
Here is a summary of some commands that are useful on Linux and Windows platforms.

37
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:15,480
Many instances of OS command injection are blind vulnerabilities.

38
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:22,380
This means that the application does not return the output from the command within its HTTP response.

39
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:27,740
Blind vulnerabilities can still be exploited, but different techniques are required.

40
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:31,680
Enter a website that lets users submit feedback about the site.

41
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:35,120
The user enters their email address and feedback message.

42
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:39,980
The server-side application then generates an email to the site administrator containing the

43
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:41,160
feedback.

44
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:46,480
To do this, it calls out to the mail program with the submitted details.

45
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:52,100
The output from the mail command is not returned in the application's responses, and so using the

46
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:55,100
echo payload would not be effective.

47
00:03:55,100 --> 00:04:04,760
In this situation, you can use a variety of other techniques to detect and exploit a vulnerability.

48
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:09,940
You can use an injected command that will trigger a time delay, allowing you to confirm that the

49
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:15,020
command was executed based on the time that the application takes to respond.

50
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:21,340
The ping command is an effective way to do this, because it lets you specify the number of ICMP

51
00:04:21,340 --> 00:04:26,080
packets to send, and therefore the time taken for the command to run.

52
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:35,900
This command will cause the application to ping its loopback network adapter for 10 seconds.

53
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:40,860
You can redirect the output from the injected command into a file within the web root that

54
00:04:40,860 --> 00:04:43,340
you can then retrieve using your browser.

55
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:49,280
For example, this input will inject a command and redirect the output to a file.

56
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:54,440
The greater than character sends the output from the command to the specified file.

57
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:59,700
You can then use your browser to retrieve the file and view the output from the injected command.

58
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:09,000
You can use an injected command that will trigger a network interaction with a system that you

59
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,880
control using out-of-band techniques.

60
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:20,100
This payload uses the NSLOOKUP command to cause a DNS lookup for the specified domain.

61
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:25,140
The attacker can monitor for the specified lookup occurring, and therefore detect the command

62
00:05:25,140 --> 00:05:27,220
was successfully injected.

63
00:05:27,220 --> 00:05:33,760
The out-of-band channel also provides an easy way to exfiltrate the output from injected commands.

64
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:38,620
This input will cause a DNS lookup to the attacker's domain containing the result of

65
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:43,680
the injected command.

66
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:49,760
A variety of shell meta-characters can be used to perform OS command injection attacks.

67
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:56,020
A number of characters function as command separators, allowing commands to be chained together.

68
00:05:56,020 --> 00:06:01,060
These command separators work on both Windows and Unix-based systems.

69
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:05,840
And these command separators work only on Unix-based systems.

70
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:11,900
On Unix-based systems, you can also use backticks or the dollar character to perform in-line execution

71
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:16,000
of an injected command within the original command.

72
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,620
Note that the different shell meta-characters have subtly different behaviors that might affect

73
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:25,920
whether they work in certain situations, and whether they allow in-band retrieval of command

74
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:29,880
output or are useful only for blind exploitation.

75
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:35,540
Sometimes, the input that you control appears within quotation marks in the original command.

76
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:42,580
In this situation, you need to terminate the quoted context before using suitable shell meta-characters

77
00:06:42,580 --> 00:06:48,420
to inject a new command.

78
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:53,540
By far the most effective way to prevent OS command injection vulnerabilities is to never

79
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:57,460
call out to OS commands from application layer code.

80
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:02,580
In virtually every case, there are alternate ways of implementing the required functionality

81
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:05,800
using safer platform APIs.

82
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:11,400
If it is considered unavoidable to call out to OS commands with user-supplied input, then

83
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:14,620
strong input validation must be performed.

84
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:18,700
You can validate against a whitelist of permitted values.

85
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:21,720
You can validate that the input is a number.

86
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:26,620
You can validate that the input contains only alphanumeric characters, no other syntax or

87
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:28,620
whitespace.

88
00:07:28,620 --> 00:07:33,060
Never attempt to sanitize input by escaping shell meta-characters.

89
00:07:33,060 --> 00:07:38,860
In practice, this is just too error-prone and vulnerable to being bypassed by a skilled attacker.

